 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Piecewise-Linear Classification](881-piecewise-linear-classification.md)
-A local classification technique that maps voxel intensity and derived features (such as gradient magnitude) to RGBA optical properties using piecewise-linear functions, commonly called ramps. It solves the problem of emphasizing specific data features by allowing users to define smooth transitions between material classes, distinguishing it from the hard thresholds of [Binary Classification](931-binary-classification.md) and enabling multi-channel control unlike basic single-channel methods. It is a specific instance of [Binary and Soft Classification](6229-binary-and-soft-classification.md) that relies on linear interpolation between user-defined keypoints. See [Piecewise-Linear Classification](881-piecewise-linear-classification.md).
+A local classification technique that maps voxel intensity and derived features (such as gradient magnitude) to RGBA optical properties using piecewise-linear functions, commonly called ramps. It solves the problem of emphasizing specific data features by mapping scalar values to visual properties. It is a specific instance of this family that relies on linear interpolation between user-defined keypoints. It sets itself apart from [Binary Classification](931-binary-classification.md) by allowing gradual opacity changes rather than hard thresholds. Choose it when precise control over material boundaries and smooth transitions is required. See [Piecewise-Linear Classification](881-piecewise-linear-classification.md).
 
 ### [Binary Classification](931-binary-classification.md)
-A volume rendering method that reduces memory consumption and accelerates rendering by categorizing input volume voxels into two distinct groups: "1-voxels" (included) and "0-voxels" (discarded) based on a threshold criterion. Unlike soft or piecewise-linear methods that assign continuous opacity values, this technique makes discrete "yes" or "no" decisions, creating sharp transitions in the output. It sets itself apart from siblings like [Piecewise-Linear Classification](881-piecewise-linear-classification.md) by eliminating intermediate opacity values, thereby enabling significant speedups through voxel culling, albeit at the cost of introducing high-frequency spectral artifacts. See [Binary Classification](931-binary-classification.md).
+A volume rendering method that reduces memory consumption and accelerates rendering by categorizing input volume voxels into two distinct groups: "0-voxels" (excluded) and "1-voxels" (included) based on a threshold. It functions as a hard decision process, creating sharp transitions in the output opacity and color. It sets itself apart from [Piecewise-Linear Classification](881-piecewise-linear-classification.md) by producing a binary mask of the object of interest, effectively discarding background data to enable real-time performance on large datasets. Choose it when maximum rendering speed and simple foreground/background separation are priorities. See [Binary Classification](931-binary-classification.md).
 
 ### [Multiple Material Classification](6343-multiple-material-classification.md)
-Extends single-material classification techniques to identify and render distinct substances within a volume simultaneously, allowing users to assign specific opacity and color properties to different classes (e.g., high opacity for an organ, low opacity for context). It differs from [Binary Classification](931-binary-classification.md) by supporting more than two distinct material definitions and from [Piecewise-Linear Classification](881-piecewise-linear-classification.md) by managing multiple independent classification models rather than a single unified transfer function. See [Multiple Material Classification](6343-multiple-material-classification.md).
+A technique that classifies more than one material in a volume by employing multiple independent classifiers, allowing distinct opacity and color assignments for different tissue types or objects. It solves the problem of visualizing complex scenes where multiple materials with overlapping or similar intensity ranges must be rendered simultaneously. It sets itself apart from [Binary Classification](931-binary-classification.md) by supporting arbitrary numbers of material classes rather than a single binary distinction, and from [Piecewise-Linear Classification](881-piecewise-linear-classification.md) by structuring the classification process as a composition of separate, sequentially trained classifiers rather than a single unified transfer function. Choose it when rendering complex multi-material scenes like medical volumes with overlapping tissues. See [Multiple Material Classification](6343-multiple-material-classification.md).
 
 ### Non-Binary Classifier
-A CRIO process that smoothly transitions from 'no' to 'yes' with varying degrees of acceptance, generating soft edges [S1, unofficial]. It applies smooth transitions between regions of interest and no interest, avoiding sharp corners in the classifier function [S1, unofficial]. Unlike binary classifiers, it introduces significantly fewer high frequencies, reducing aliasing artifacts [S1, unofficial].
+A classification process that smoothly transitions from 'no' to 'yes' with varying degrees of acceptance, generating soft edges [S1, unofficial]. It applies smooth transitions between regions of interest and no interest, avoiding sharp corners in the classifier function [S1, unofficial]. Unlike binary classifiers, it introduces significantly fewer high frequencies, reducing aliasing artifacts [S1, unofficial].
+
+### Classification Probability (2D TF)
+A method to separate tissues with overlapping intensity ranges by using a second TF dimension derived from local histogram analysis. This dimension represents the probability or likelihood that a voxel belongs to a specific tissue class, enabling 2D Transfer Function design [S2]. It derives neighborhood characteristics (local histogram properties) for each voxel to perform fuzzy classification of tissues in overlapping intensity ranges [S2]. It constructs the second TF dimension as a linear interpolation of tissue-specific trapezoids. This avoids added complexity for the user while extending TF power [S2].
 
